in this lesson we're going to talk about  volume of cylinders so  usually when they give these type of  problems on the act they'll give you the  formula for the volume of a cylinder but  it's good just to know how to work with  the formula and understand what it  represents and and so on so let's just  kind of go through this real quick here  a cylinder basically is like a a can  like a can of pop or soda like that and  the volume v is equal to pi which of  course is the 3.14 times the radius  squared times the height okay so the  height is the distance between the two  circles the two circular bases and the  radius is the  distance from the center to a point on  that circle there so let's go through an  example see if you can do this one on  your own and we'll go through it  together it says if the volume of a  cylinder is 200 cubic centimeters and  the height is 10 centimeters what is the  radius of the base so see if you can do  that one on your own and we'll go  through it together here so we've got  volume equals pi r squared h i usually  like to write the formula down first and  then substitute in what you know so 200  equals pi  times r squared times the height which  is 10. so i'm just going to work from  the outside in towards uh towards r so  i'm going to divide both sides by pi  and 10  okay so i'll just say 10 pi so that way  those cancel and so now we're down to  let's see let's reduce this a little bit  so 10 goes in here 20 times so we have  20 divided by pi  equals r squared and then if we take the  square root because we're trying to  cancel that out we want to do that to  both sides so the radius is the square  root of 20 over pi that's an exact  answer  you could do it on your calculator and  get a decimal approximation but i  basically just wanted to make sure that  you're familiar with the volume of  cylinders this problem you know they  have something with a cylinder on the  test in some form generally and so again  it's just good to know the 